+---
+id: dl-001-rental-module-in-memory-storage-half-open-day-intervals-counter-based-booking-ids
+type: decision-log
+title: "Rental module: in-memory storage, half-open day intervals, counter-based booking ids"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+The first version of the booking desk's rental module (`createRentalService()`) needed a storage model,
+a way to represent "whole UTC days" periods, and a way to generate booking ids, while staying inside the
+project's no-runtime-dependency and determinism constraints.
+
+## Decision
+
+- **Storage**: equipment and bookings are kept in plain in-memory `Map`s inside the closure returned by
+  `createRentalService()`, matching the request to "keep everything in memory" with no persistence layer.
+- **Periods**: a rental period is represented as a half-open interval `[start, end)` of UTC-midnight
+  timestamps. `start` and `end` must both be exactly midnight UTC and `end` must be strictly after
+  `start`; the number of days is `(end - start) / 86 400 000`. Two bookings on the same item overlap when
+  `aStart < bEnd && bStart < aEnd`, which lets back-to-back bookings (one ending exactly when the next
+  starts) coexist without being treated as overlapping.
+- **Booking ids**: an incrementing in-closure counter (`"1"`, `"2"`, …), not `Math.random()` or
+  timestamps, to keep the service deterministic and keep booking ids reproducible in tests, per the
+  project's determinism directive.
+- **Validation scope**: `addEquipment` rejects a duplicate id, an invalid `kind`, and a non-integer or
+  negative `dailyPrice`, since these are external inputs crossing a system boundary. `quote`/`book`
+  validate the period and the item's existence; `book` reuses `quote` internally to avoid duplicating
+  that validation.
+- **Tooling**: no `typescript`, `ts-node`/`tsx`, or linter dependency was added. Node 22.23's native TS
+  type-stripping already runs `src/index.test.ts` and `src/index.ts` directly under `node --test`,
+  consistent with the project's "no runtime dependency" description; introducing build tooling was out of
+  scope for this request.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Booking ids are only unique within a single service instance / process lifetime (expected, since
+  state is in-memory and not persisted).
+- Because there is no linter configured in the repository, the code-quality directive's "linter reports
+  no errors" rule is currently vacuously satisfied; types were reviewed by hand in the absence of an
+  installed `tsc`. A future change that adds real persistence or a linter should revisit this.
+- Cancellation (rule 5) is not implemented; `book`/`quote` only cover creation, as instructed.
